Tomcat Manager, Roles, and RemoteAddrValve Explained

1.  Why do we comment out the Valve section?

By default, the Tomcat Manager and Host Manager applications can only be
accessed from the local machine (localhost).

Example:

The RemoteAddrValve checks the client’s IP address. The allow attribute
permits only: - 127.0.0.1 (IPv4 localhost) - ::1 (IPv6 localhost)

If Jenkins or your browser tries to access the Manager application from
another EC2 instance, Tomcat blocks the request.

By commenting out the Valve, remote systems such as Jenkins can access
the Manager application.

Interview Answer: We comment out the RemoteAddrValve to allow remote
access to the Tomcat Manager application. By default, it only allows
connections from localhost for security reasons.

